**Verdict:** At 2ct, lab isn't just cheaper — it's the only realistic option under $5K. A 2ct natural marquise costs over $22K. A 2ct D VVS1 lab costs $2,403.

## GIA vs IGI: What Certification Means for Each

Natural [marquise diamonds](/marquise-cut-diamond) are certified by [GIA](https://www.gia.edu/). Lab-grown marquise diamonds are primarily certified by [IGI](https://www.igi.org/).

**GIA (natural):**
- Most conservative grading lab
- A GIA VS2 is a true VS2 — grade inflation is minimal
- GIA adds 20–30% to natural resale value vs uncertified stones
- GIA does not give Cut grades for marquise — only Polish and Symmetry

**IGI (lab):**
- Standard certification for lab-grown diamonds
- Slightly less conservative than GIA — IGI VS2 and GIA VS2 may not be equivalent
- Widely accepted across all certified retailers
- IGI grades DO include Cut for fancy shapes including marquise

**For buyers:** A GIA natural G VS2 and an IGI lab D VVS2 at the same price tier are not equivalent grades — the IGI may be graded slightly more generously. For natural stones, insist on GIA. For lab stones, IGI is the accepted standard.

See our full [GIA certified marquise diamond guide](/gia-certified-marquise-diamond) for how to read the certificate on either stone type.

## Marquise Shape: Does Origin Change the Look?

No. The marquise shape behaves identically in natural and lab stones.

The same four proportion rules apply regardless of origin:
- **L/W ratio**: 1.85–2.00:1 for classic marquise proportion
- **Depth %**: 58–62% for full face-up size
- **Table %**: 53–63% for balanced brilliance
- **Color**: G-H in white metal; H-I in yellow gold

The bowtie shadow appears in both natural and lab stones. It's a function of cut geometry, not origin. A well-cut lab marquise has less bowtie than a poorly-cut natural marquise.

With a [marquise diamond wedding band](/marquise-diamond-wedding-band), origin doesn't change setting compatibility. The stone sits identically in the head.

> **"I get this question constantly: does the lab stone look different in the ring? The honest answer is no — not unless you hold it under a spectroscope. I've set hundreds of both, and the only difference is the price tag I paid. A 2ct D VVS1 lab marquise in a platinum bezel looks extraordinary. A client who doesn't know it's lab won't guess. I have clients who chose lab specifically because the savings paid for a better setting, a better [bezel setting](/bezel-setting-diamond), better metalwork. The stone's origin isn't visible. The quality of the setting is."**
> — Farzana Hasan, diamond expert | [About Farzana](/about-farzana/)

## What "Lab-Grown" Means on the GIA or IGI Report

Buy a lab [marquise diamond](/marquise-cut-diamond) with a GIA or IGI cert. The report looks identical to a natural cert — except one line. GIA reports include the words "Laboratory-Grown" in the title. IGI reports include "Lab-Grown Diamond" and note the growth method (CVD or HPHT).

CVD (Chemical Vapor Deposition) and HPHT (High Pressure High Temperature) are the two growth methods. Both produce identical carbon crystal structures. Both receive the same grading standards.

The cert still shows:
- Carat weight
- Color grade
- Clarity grade
- Cut, Polish, Symmetry (IGI grades Cut for fancy shapes; GIA does not)
- Fluorescence
- Measurements and proportions

Everything you check for natural, you check for lab. Our [GIA certified marquise guide](/gia-certified-marquise-diamond) covers all four cert fields — color, clarity, L/W ratio, depth/table %. They apply to both origins.

